                            <a name="comment2846"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Two Lives Blurred
</h4>

<p>
Thanks for recommending this, Kruse. It's a terrific piece of writing. He tells a great story and manages to give readers a window into the world of photographers and combatants. I thought I'd reached my limit of PTSD stories, but this one got to me.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="www.jsonline.com">Mark Johnson</a>
	

 at November 13,2007 13:22
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2847"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Two Lives Blurred
</h4>

<p>
I've got to admit, I had no idea where this story was going, but the simplicity made it very easy to keep reading. If his photography career falls through, he can always write.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:dirkchatelain@hotmail.com">Dirk</a>
	

 at November 13,2007 15:30
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2848"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Two Lives Blurred
</h4>

<p>
I watched the photo essay first, read the story later. What struck me was the different points of view, the first being the soldier's the second being the journalist's. What struck me more was the equal weight of the stories' impacts on me.  Both versions remind us that you don't have to be a writer to tell a pretty f--in powerful narrative. All you have to be is a red-blooded human with a desire to say honestly the things you feel and experience. Again, wow. (Thanks to Kruse for the read.) 
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


